    "taxonId": 56992,
    "summary": "Low-growing annual, rarely a short-lived perennial, native to the Mediterranean Basin and Macaronesia. It bears dense clusters of small, honey-scented flowers, and its petals, leaves and tender stems can be eaten raw or cooked.",
    "humanEdibility": "yes",
    "edibilityNote": "Petals, leaves and tender stems can be eaten raw or cooked.",
    "evidence": {
      "summary": ["s1:p4", "s1:p5", "s1:p6", "s1:p10"],
      "humanEdibility": ["s1:p10"]
    }
  },
  {
    "taxonId": 83375,
    "summary": "Perennial herb native to eastern Australia, with long, strap-like leaves arising from underground stems. Its leaf tip has three prominent points, with the side points as long as or longer than the middle one. Seeds may be ground into flour, while the flowers and leaf bases are also edible.",
    "humanEdibility": "yes",
    "edibilityNote": "Seeds may be ground into flour for Indigenous Australian damper; flowers and leaf bases are also eaten, with leaf bases chewed for water.",
    "evidence": {
      "summary": ["s1:p1", "s1:p2", "s1:p4", "s1:p5"],
      "humanEdibility": ["s1:p5"]
    }
  },
  {
    "taxonId": 162167,
    "summary": "Perennial herb native to North America, especially the Great Plains of central Canada and the central United States. Its stems and leaves are hairy, and each long flower stalk ends in a head of pink or purple outer florets surrounding numerous orange florets.",
    "humanEdibility": "unknown",
    "edibilityNote": null,
    "evidence": {
      "summary": ["s1:p1", "s1:p2"],
      "humanEdibility": []
    }
  },
  {
    "taxonId": 346424,
    "summary": "Woody climber endemic to eastern Australia. It has leaves divided into several egg-shaped leaflets and white or pink trumpet-shaped flowers that are red and hairy inside.",
    "humanEdibility": "unknown",
    "edibilityNote": null,
    "evidence": {
      "summary": ["s1:p2"],
      "humanEdibility": []
    }
  },
  {
    "taxonId": 82303,
    "summary": "Deciduous fern native to Europe, North Africa and West Asia, growing in woodland bogs and beside streams. Its stout underground stems can eventually form a woody, trunk-like base, while brown spore-bearing tips make some fronds resemble flowers.",
    "humanEdibility": "yes",
    "edibilityNote": "In some places, young shoots called fiddleheads are eaten and said to taste like asparagus; the source marks this claim citation needed.",
    "evidence": {
      "summary": ["s1:p1", "s1:p2"],
      "humanEdibility": ["s1:p15"]
    }
  },
  {
    "taxonId": 119302,
    "summary": "Slow-growing succulent perennial native to Arizona, New Mexico and northern Mexico. Its grey-green leaves retain impressions from earlier leaves and end in a contrasting dark spine. A mature plant produces one tall stalk of bright yellow flowers and then dies.",
    "humanEdibility": "unknown",
    "edibilityNote": null,
    "evidence": {
      "summary": ["s1:p1", "s1:p2", "s1:p4"],
      "humanEdibility": []
    }
  },
  {
    "taxonId": 136037,
    "summary": "Evergreen tree-like cycad of unspecified native origin, found around Madagascar, nearby western Indian Ocean islands and parts of coastal East Africa. Its young leaves have a distinctive bluish tint, and its seeds contain a spongy layer that lets them float. Although toxic, the seeds are eaten on Ngazidja after repeated washing, fermentation and drying remove their cyanotoxins.",
    "humanEdibility": "yes",
    "edibilityNote": "On Ngazidja, toxic seeds are made edible by repeated washing, fermentation and drying to remove cyanotoxins.",
    "evidence": {
      "summary": ["s1:p1", "s1:p2", "s1:p3", "s1:p8"],
      "humanEdibility": ["s1:p8"]
    }
  },
  {
    "taxonId": 81797,
    "summary": "Perennial ornamental plant native to the United States, Canada and northeastern Mexico. Its hairy, grass-like leaves surround shorter stems bearing yellow, star-shaped flowers, which open in the morning and usually wilt in hot afternoon sun; despite its appearance, it is not a grass.",
    "humanEdibility": "unknown",
    "edibilityNote": null,
    "evidence": {
      "summary": ["s1:p1", "s1:p2", "s1:p3"],
      "humanEdibility": []
    }
  },
  {
    "taxonId": 163999,
    "summary": "Moss of unspecified native origin, widely distributed in Europe and the Aleutian Islands and also found elsewhere. It showed intermediate sensitivity to the herbicide Asulam in a study of moss growth.",
    "humanEdibility": "unknown",
    "edibilityNote": null,
    "evidence": {
      "summary": ["s1:p1", "s1:p2"],
